;; Lesson 3: a ramp and a rolling ball (#184). Read by src/HeroicInventions.Sim/Editor/Lessons.cs;
;; the format is described there.
;;
;; The numbers, worked by hand before writing the steps (the starter ramp: 1 m long, 15 degrees, a
;; slab 5 cm thick; the starter ball: iron, 5 cm in radius; racket/machines/ball-ramp.rkt works out
;; the same law for two bigger balls):
;;   a solid ball has I = 2/5 m r², so a share 2/7 of the energy it loses goes into turning it:
;;     a = g sin(theta) / (1 + 2/5) = 5/7 g sin(theta) = 0.7143 × 9.81 × 0.2588 = 1.814 m/s² along the slope
;;     (a block sliding with no friction would get g sin(theta) = 2.539)
;;   the ball is put 0.9 m up the slope, standing off the slab's middle by half its thickness and its radius,
;;   0.025 + 0.05 + 0.001 = 0.076 m, so its centre is at
;;     y = 0.9 sin 15° + 0.076 cos 15° = 0.2329 + 0.0734 = 0.3063 m
;;     z = -0.9 cos 15° + 0.076 sin 15° = -0.8693 + 0.0197 = -0.8496 m (the ramp rises toward -z)
;;   it comes to rest on the ground with its centre one radius, 0.05 m, up. It gives up h = 0.3063 - 0.05 = 0.2563 m:
;;     v = sqrt(10/7 g h) = sqrt(1.4286 × 9.81 × 0.2563) = 1.895 m/s   (a sliding block: sqrt(2 g h) = 2.243 m/s)
;;     it turns at w = v / r = 1.895 / 0.05 = 37.9 rad/s, under the engine's cap of 47.1 rad/s
;;     (47.1 × 0.05 = 2.36 m/s is as fast as a 5 cm ball can roll; that is a drop of 0.40 m, so keep the ramp low)
;;   the ball leaves the slab's lip and drops the last 0.023 m onto the ground, still rolling.
;;   Traced in the sim (an editor Test of this very rig): the fastest the ball goes is 1.910 m/s, 0.8 % above the worked
;;   number; nothing slows a ball rolling on flat ground, so it carries on at that speed for the whole 30 s test (52 m).
;; So the check: the ball's fastest speed is within 0.15 m/s of 1.895, and it ended at least 1 m from where it began.

(lesson ramp
  (title "Lesson 3: the ramp and the ball")
  (view 1.3 0.35 3.2 0 0.2 -0.5)

  (step
    (text "Put a ramp down. Pick Ramp in the parts list (it is lit up), then click on the green shape in the scene. A ramp is a fixed slope; its low end goes on the ground and it rises toward the back.")
    (highlight ramp)
    (target (kind ramp) (at 0 0 0) (within 0.5))
    (check place (kind ramp) (as ramp)))

  (step
    (text "Put a ball near the top of the ramp, on the green ball. Pick Ball: it is iron, and heavy for its size.")
    (highlight ball)
    (target (kind ball) (on ramp) (along 0.9) (up 0.076) (within 0.2))
    (check place (kind ball) (as ball)))

  (step
    (text "Work out what the ball will do before you watch it. Its middle is {drop} above the ground it will stop on, and it gives up that height as it goes down. A block sliding on a slope with no friction would turn all of it into speed: {slide} at the bottom. A ball turns some of it into spinning, so it only reaches {speed}: a solid ball keeps back two sevenths of what it loses to spin, which is why it is slower than a block. Press Test it (lit, top left): the ball rolls down and away.")
    (highlight test)
    (fail "The ball's fastest was {top} and it ended {across} from where it began, but rolling down from {drop} up should give {speed}, and it should roll away at least a metre. Is the ball on the ramp, near the top? Move it back to the green ball and press Test it again.")
    (check rolled (part ball) (far 1) (within 0.15)))

  (done "The ball reached {top} (worked out beforehand: {speed}) and rolled on {across}: nothing slows a ball on smooth flat ground. A block with no friction would have reached {slide}. A ball spins as it goes, and the spinning takes a share of the energy, so on any slope it picks up speed at five sevenths of what a sliding block would: 1.81 metres a second faster every second here, against 2.54."))
